<GuideLayout slug="broward-county-tree-removal-permit">
  <p class="note">
    <strong>Short answer:</strong> in Broward County, the rules depend on where the property is. Most cities, including
    Fort Lauderdale, Hollywood, Pembroke Pines, Pompano Beach and Coral Springs, run their own tree permit process. The
    county's Tree Preservation Program handles unincorporated Broward and a few cities. A dangerous tree on a
    single-family home can be exempt when a certified arborist documents the risk.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 1: find out who regulates your property</h2>
  <p>
    Start with your address. If it is inside a city, that city's tree ordinance applies. If you live in an unincorporated
    area (the Broward Municipal Services District), or in one of the municipalities that rely on the county, the county's
    Tree Preservation Program applies. The county's own materials list cities such as Lauderdale-by-the-Sea, Tamarac and
    Wilton Manors among those it covers. If you are unsure, ask the county or your city before you cut.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 2: county tree removal license</h2>
  <p>
    The county's Tree Preservation Program requires a <strong>Tree Removal License</strong> for removing trees with a
    trunk diameter of about 3 inches or more (measured 4.5 feet above the ground), with exemptions that include nuisance
    trees, hazardous trees and trees near power lines. The county's FAQ for this rule was last updated in 2019, so call
    the program at 954-519-1483 to confirm the current details for your tree.
  </p>

  <h2>Cities: each has its own rules</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/areas/fort-lauderdale-tree-removal/">Fort Lauderdale</a>, <a href="/areas/hollywood-tree-service/">Hollywood</a> and <a href="/areas/pembroke-pines-tree-removal/">Pembroke Pines</a> have their own ordinances and permit process.</li>
    <li><a href="/areas/pompano-beach-tree-removal/">Pompano Beach</a> requires a tree permit before many removals under its zoning code, and its rules limit topping and severe pruning.</li>
    <li><a href="/areas/coral-springs-tree-removal/">Coral Springs</a> has a residential tree removal permit for single-family and duplex properties that asks for HOA approval and can require replacement trees.</li>
    <li>Other cities, including <a href="/areas/miramar-tree-removal/">Miramar</a>, <a href="/areas/davie-tree-removal/">Davie</a> and <a href="/areas/deerfield-beach-tree-removal/">Deerfield Beach</a>, set their own requirements. Always check with the city.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The state exemption for dangerous trees</h2>
  <p>
    Under Florida Statute 163.045, a local government may not require a permit, fee or replacement tree to prune, trim or
    remove a tree on <em>residential property</em> (a detached single-family home) if the owner has documentation from an
    ISA certified arborist or a Florida licensed landscape architect that the tree poses an unacceptable risk to people
    or property. The assessment must follow the ISA tree risk assessment method, and the bar is high: removal has to be
    the practical way to bring the risk down. Keep the signed report. See <a href="/certified-arborist/">arborist
    assessments</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Trimming, licenses and topping</h2>
  <p>
    Broward County no longer requires tree trimming contractors to hold a county Tree Trimmer license (the requirement
    ended on July 1, 2025), but the county still enforces its tree abuse rules, and trimming must follow ANSI standards.
    Topping and similar practices are prohibited, and some cities may still add their own requirements for trimmers. See
    our page on <a href="/tree-trimming/">tree trimming</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What happens if you skip the permit</h2>
  <p>
    Cities and the county can issue fines and require replacement trees, often several for each tree removed. The cost is
    usually far higher than the permit. Also check your HOA: its approval is separate from the city's.
  </p>

  <h2>Next step</h2>
  <p>
    Not sure what applies to your tree? <a href="/contact-us/">Request a free estimate</a> and ask the professional you
    are connected with what is usually required for your address. For general rules across South Florida, read our
    <a href="/tree-removal-permits/">permit guide</a>.
  </p>

  <p class="text-base text-text-muted">
    This is general information, not legal advice, and rules change. Details checked in October 2026 against the
    <a href="https://broward.org/Environment/TreePreservation/Pages/TreeLicenses.aspx">Broward County Tree Preservation
    Program</a>, the <a href="https://www.broward.org/Building/TreeTrimmers/Pages/Default.aspx">county's tree trimmer
    page</a> and <a href="https://m.flsenate.gov/Statutes/163.045">Florida Statute 163.045</a>.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
